+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Each submission names the base revision it started from — the revision ID the worker's tablet had loaded before editing. A submission whose base revision matches the visit's current accepted revision becomes the new current revision. A submission whose base revision does not match the current accepted revision (because another edit was accepted first, or because another submission is already contesting the same base) becomes a competing revision: it does not become current, and the visit carries unresolved review work until a coordinator resolves it.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Two or more revisions competing on the same base are never combined automatically, regardless of which fields they touch. This holds even when the competing edits touch unrelated fields, trading a larger review queue for the certainty that no combination happens without a coordinator seeing it.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+## Submission and Retrieval
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolution. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+A submission envelope carries: the visit ID, the base revision ID, the author ID, an idempotency key, and the full visit payload (plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes). The payload is always the worker's complete visit, never a partial change set.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+Current-state retrieval returns the visit's current accepted revision (its revision ID and full payload) and an indicator of whether unresolved review work exists for that visit. While review work is unresolved, retrieval also exposes the competing revisions contesting the shared base — each one's revision ID, author, full payload, and server receipt time — so a coordinator (via review-export, designed later) can compare them.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Resolution
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+A coordinator resolves unresolved review work by submitting: the visit ID, the contributing submission IDs (the competing revisions the resolution draws on), and the resulting payload. The resulting payload may be identical to one of the contributing revisions or composed by the coordinator from more than one of them — the record store does not interpret how the coordinator arrived at it, only records the outcome. The server stores this as a new revision, attributes it to the coordinator, links it to the contributing submission IDs, and makes it the visit's current accepted revision. All original submissions remain retained regardless of resolution.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|--------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for independently edited visits | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions go to coordinator review; no automatic combination | Field-aware three-way merge: disjoint field edits combine automatically, overlapping edits go to review | Automatic field merging cannot see scientific relationships between fields the server cannot infer from field names (e.g., a species identification and its cover estimate) — it risks silently combining edits that are related in substance but not in name. A larger review queue is an acceptable, visible cost; a silent incorrect merge is not. |
